  "source_title": "v1 canon. Chinese text via ctext.org (Si Shu Zhang Ju Ji Zhu). English translation: James Legge (public domain), seeded as the sole canonical translation per line. Modern copyrighted translations (Lau/Slingerland/Watson) are referenced by the platform, not stored. Source-of-truth file in this repo: content/SOURCES.md.",
  "blurb": "Laozi's 81 short chapters on the dao — the other half of classical Chinese thought, in compressed riddles.",
  "background": "The Daodejing (Tao Te Ching) is the foundational text of Daoism — a short work of 81 brief chapters traditionally attributed to Laozi, a figure so shadowy that some scholars read the text as a multi-author compilation that crystallized around 400 BCE. Where Confucius asks how to live in the world, the Daodejing asks why you'd want to: it champions wuwei (often translated 'non-action' but closer to 'effortless action'), the uncarved block, the valley spirit, the usefulness of emptiness. The style is gnomic and paradoxical — 'the way that can be spoken of is not the constant way' — and almost every line has generated centuries of commentary. It is one of the most translated books in the world, and the translations diverge sharply (the received text itself has at least three major manuscript traditions, including the Guodian bamboo slips from c. 300 BCE). English seeding is James Legge (public domain); modern alternatives (D.C. Lau, Arthur Waley, Ames & Hall, Mair) are referenced, not stored.",
